"We start with the given equations:\n\\[ x^2 + y^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ x^4 + y^4 = \\frac{17}{18} \\]\n\nFirst, we square the first equation:\n\\[ (x^2 + y^2)^2 = 1^2 \\]\n\\[ x^4 + 2x^2y^2 + y^4 = 1 \\]\n\nWe already know \\( x^4 + y^4 = \\frac{17}{18} \\), so we substitute this into the equation:\n\\[ \\frac{17}{18} + 2x^2y^2 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 2x^2y^2 = 1 - \\frac{17}{18} \\]\n\\[ 2x^2y^2 = \\frac{1}{18} \\]\n\\[ x^2y^2 = \\frac{1}{36} \\]\n\nSince \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are positive, we take the square root of both sides:\n\\[ xy = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}} \\]"
"We need to find the value of \\( c \\) such that the quadratic expression \\( x^2 + cx + 9c \\) can be written as the square of a binomial. Let's assume that the square of the binomial is \\( (x + a)^2 \\).\n\nExpanding \\( (x + a)^2 \\), we get:\n\\[\n(x + a)^2 = x^2 + 2ax + a^2\n\\]\n\nWe need this to equal \\( x^2 + cx + 9c \\). Therefore, we equate the coefficients of the corresponding terms:\n\\[\na = 3c \\quad \\text{(from the constant term)}\n\\]\n\\[\n2a = c \\quad \\text{(from the coefficient of } x\\text{)}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( a = 3c \\), we substitute \\( a \\) in the second equation:\n\\[\n2(3c) = c\n\\]\n\\[\n6c = c\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( c \\), we get:\n\\[\n5c = c\n\\]\n\\[\nc = 0\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( c \\) is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."
