"To determine the number of ways to choose a President, Vice-President, and Treasurer from a group of 8 people, where no one can serve two roles at the same time, we need to consider the order in which the positions are filled.\n\n1. **Choosing the President:**\n   There are 8 possible choices for the President.\n\n2. **Choosing the Vice-President:**\n   After choosing the President, there are 7 remaining choices for the Vice-President.\n\n3. **Choosing the Treasurer:**\n   After choosing the President and Vice-President, there are 6 remaining choices for the Treasurer.\n\nTo find the total number of ways to assign these three positions, we multiply the number of choices for each position:\n\n\\[\n8 \\text{ (choices for President)} \\times 7 \\text{ (choices for Vice-President)} \\times 6 \\text{ (choices for Treasurer)}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this gives:\n\n\\[\n8 \\times 7 \\times 6 = 336\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to choose a President, Vice-President, and a Treasurer from a group of 8 people is \\(\\boxed{336}\\)."
"To determine the number of different sandwiches that can be ordered, we need to calculate the number of ways to choose 2 fillings out of the 9 available. This is a combination problem, where the order of selection does not matter.\n\nThe formula for combinations is given by:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{r} = \\frac{n!}{r!(n-r)!}\n\\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to choose from, and \\( r \\) is the number of items to choose.\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 9 \\) and \\( r = 2 \\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9!}{2!(9-2)!} = \\frac{9!}{2! \\cdot 7!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{2 \\times 1 \\times 7!} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2 \\times 1} = \\frac{72}{2} = 36\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different sandwiches that can be ordered is \\( \\boxed{36}\\)."
