"First, we need to convert 1 pound and 5 pence into farthings.\n\n1. Convert 1 pound to farthings:\n   - 1 pound = 20 shillings\n   - 1 shilling = 12 pence\n   - Therefore, 1 pound = 20 shillings * 12 pence/shilling = 2440 farthings\n\n2. Convert 5 pence to farthings:\n   - 1 pence = 4 farthings\n   - Therefore, 5 pence = 5 pence * 4 farthings/pence = 20 farthings\n\n3. Add the farthings from the pound and the pence:\n   - Farthings from the pound = 2440 farthings\n   - Farthings from 5 pence = 20 farthings\n   - Total farthings = 2440 farthings + 20 farthings = 2460 farthings\n\nTherefore, the total number of farthings is $\\boxed{2460}$.\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $2460$. I hope it is correct."
"To determine the \\(2007^{\\text{th}}\\) digit to the right of the decimal point in the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\), we first need to find the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\).\n\nPerform the division \\(1 \\div 7\\):\n\n\\[\n1 \\div 7 = 0.\\overline{142887}\n\\]\n\nThe decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is the repeating sequence \\(142887\\), which has a length of 6 digits.\n\nTo find the \\(2007^{\\text{th}}\\) digit, we need to determine the position within the repeating sequence. We do this by finding the remainder when 2007 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n2007 \\div 6 = 334 \\text{ remainder } 3\n\\]\n\nThe remainder is 3, which means the \\(2007^{\\text{th}}\\) digit corresponds to the 3rd digit in the repeating sequence \\(142887\\). This digit is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]"
"To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\(1313\\) and \\(1001\\), we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of \\(0\\).\n\n1. Divide \\(1313\\) by \\(1001\\) and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   1313 \\div 1001 = 1 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 312\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, divide \\(1001\\) by the remainder \\(312\\) and find the new remainder:\n   \\[\n   1001 \\div 312 = 3 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 17\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder is now \\(0\\), we stop here. The last non-zero remainder is \\(17\\).\n\nTherefore, the greatest common divisor of \\(1313\\) and \\(1001\\) is:\n\\(\\boxed{17}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(17\\). I hope it is correct."
