"To solve this problem, we can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion. Let's denote the following:\n- \\( R \\) as the set of red cars.\n- \\( W \\) as the set of white cars.\n- \\( D \\) as the set of 4-door cars.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\( |R| = 12 \\)\n- \\( |D| = 15 \\)\n- \\( |D \\cap W| = 4 \\)\n\nWe need to find \\( |R \\cap D| \\), the number of cars that are both red and 4-door.\n\nUsing the principle of inclusion-exclusion for two sets:\n\\[ |R + \\ D| = |R| + |D| - |R \\cap D| \\]\n\nWe know that all cars are either red or white, and all cars are either 2-door or 4-door. Therefore, there are no cars that are neither red nor white or neither 2-door nor 4-door. This implies that:\n\\[ |R \\cap D| = 0 \\]\n\nThus, the number of cars that are both 4-door and red is \\(\\boxed{0}\\)."
