"To determine the width of the new rectangle, we need to maintain the same proportional relationship as the original rectangle. The original rectangle has a length of 6 inches and a width of 10 inches. The new rectangle has a length of 9 inches.\n\nLet's denote the width of the new rectangle as \\( w \\).\n\nSince the rectangles are proportional, the ratio of the length to the width in the original rectangle should be the same as the ratio of the length to the width in the new rectangle. Therefore, we can set up the following proportion:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{10} = \\frac{9}{w}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( w \\), we can cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n6w = 9 \\times 10\n\\]\n\n\\[\n6w = 90\n\\]\n\nNext, we divide both sides by 6:\n\n\\[\nw = \\frac{90}{6}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nw = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the width of the new rectangle is 15 inches.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $15$. I hope it is correct."
