"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\sec x - \\tan x = 2\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(\\sec x + \\tan x\\). To do this, we can use the identity:\n\\[\n(\\sec x - \\tan x)(\\sec x + \\tan x) = \\sec^2 x - \\tan^2 x\n\\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[\n\\sec^2 x - \\tan^2 x = 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the identity becomes:\n\\[\n(\\sec x - \\tan x)(\\sec x + \\tan x) = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given value \\(\\sec x - \\tan x = 2\\):\n\\[\n2(\\sec x + \\tan x) = 1\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\sec x + \\tan x\\):\n\\[\n\\sec x + \\tan x = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sec x + \\tan x\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{ 2}}\\).\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\). I hope it is correct."
